The task requires determining the fraction of the given paragraph's value that survives after deleting all content that describes, compares, or bridges prior work. This means identifying the portion of the paragraph that is not related to prior work (i.e., the authors' original contribution).

The paragraph provided is:
> "In a multi-agent LLM system all team members may be assigned to the same task (Liu et al., 2023; Liang et al., 2023a), or they may have different roles aligned with their specific expertise or subtasks (Zhang et al., 2024a; Qian et al., 2024). Another important consideration is the agents’ adaptability: whether they can learn, adapt, or modify their strategies over time by updating internal parameters. For instance, Alfonso et al. (Amayuelas et al., 2024) demonstrated that models could be influenced to alter their behavior in ways that ultimately degrade overall system performance. Such interference may occur through direct manipulation of agents’ individual contributions or deceptive communication tactics (Amayuelas et al., 2024). Therefore, establishing robust mechanisms to mitigate adversarial threats is essential to maintaining the integrity and reliability of the system. A major benefit of our systems is the robustness against adversarial agents. Specifically, allocating agents with credibility scores, our system gradually penalizes agents with adversarial behaviors (see Section 5). In Section 6, we demonstrate that our system can tolerate even more than half of the agents being adversaries."

The citation_focus_text (the part that describes, compares, or bridges prior work) is given as:
> "Agent Roles. In a multi-agent LLM system all team members may be assigned to the same task (Liu et al., 2023; Liang et al., 2023a), or they may have different roles aligned with their specific expertise or subtasks (Zhang et al., 2024a; Qian et al., 2024). Another important consideration is the agents’ adaptability: whether they can learn, adapt, or modify their strategies over time by updating internal parameters. For instance, Alfonso et al. (Amayuelas et al., 2024) demonstrated that models could be influenced to alter their behavior in ways that ultimately degrade overall system performance. Such interference may occur through direct manipulation of agents’ individual contributions or deceptive communication tactics (Amayuelas et al., 2024). Therefore, establishing robust mechanisms to mitigate adversarial threats is essential to main-"

This citation_focus_text constitutes the content that describes, compares, or bridges prior work. The remaining part of the paragraph (the authors' original contribution) is:
> "A major benefit of our systems is the robustness against adversarial agents. Specifically, allocating agents with credibility scores, our system gradually penalizes agents with adversarial behaviors (see Section 5). In Section 6, we demonstrate that our system can tolerate even more than half of the agents being adversaries."

To find the fraction of the paragraph's value that survives after deleting the prior work content, the word count is used as a proxy for value (since the paragraph is text-based and the value is proportional to the content). 

- Total word count of the entire paragraph: approximately 120 words.
- Word count of the citation_focus_text (prior work content): approximately 80 words.
- Word count of the surviving content (authors' contribution): approximately 40 words.

The fraction of the paragraph's value that survives is:
\[
\frac{\text{Word count of surviving content}}{\text{Total word count}} = \frac{40}{120} = \frac{1}{3}
\]

This fraction represents the portion of the paragraph's value that remains after removing all content related to prior work. The surviving content is the authors' original contribution, which is the technical value of the paragraph.

